# decisions log

- 2026-08-03: franky's own `scripts/lint`/`scripts/test` heuristics can't infer commands for an sbt/Scala.js stack, so scripts/{setup,build,lint,test,serve} are hand-written (sbt compile/fastLinkJS, npm installs, scalafmt+tsc for lint, CLI registry/add smoke test for test) rather than franky-generated. `franky init --refresh-adapters` overwrote these with self-referential stubs once despite the "root scripts/<name> wins" doc guarantee — restored by hand. If refresh-adapters runs again, diff scripts/* afterward before trusting it.
- 2026-08-03: `scripts/test` intentionally does not run the full publishLocal+throwaway-sbt-project compile check (proven once manually, see AGENTS.md) — too slow for routine `franky verify`; it only asserts the CLI writes the expected files. Re-run the full check by hand before shipping anything registry/CLI-shaped.
- 2026-08-05: the 2026-08-03 entry above is now **superseded** — franky's architecture moved on since then. A later commit (`64ebe83`) relocated the real hand-written script bodies from root `scripts/` into `.franky/scripts/` (matching `.franky/base/agents/coder.md`'s current guidance: "Use `.franky/scripts/*` ... do not improvise"), but left `.franky/scripts.toml` still pointing at the deleted root paths, breaking `franky doctor`/`franky verify`. Fixed by pointing `scripts.toml` at `.franky/scripts/*`; `franky init --refresh-adapters` then correctly regenerated root `scripts/*` as thin delegating wrappers (the current-version pattern — root scripts are a stable pass-through, not the canonical implementation). Do NOT restore hand-written logic directly into root `scripts/*` — edit `.franky/scripts/*` instead and let refresh-adapters regenerate the root wrappers.
- 2026-08-05: **component fidelity pattern**, established while fixing `Alert`. A `modules/ui` component carries the *union* of (a) new-york-v4's complete, self-contained Tailwind utility string and (b) the `cn-<name>*` hook classes + `data-slot` attributes that shadcn's own `bases/{base,radix,aria}` flavor uses. Reason: `modules/ui` is what the CLI copies into consumer projects, which have neither `basecoat.generated.css` nor `shadcn-presets.generated.css`, so the utilities must stand alone; but `shadcn-presets.generated.css` (8 style packs, and the docs site always sets `data-style-pack="lyra"`) targets the `cn-*`/`data-slot` selectors. Cascade facts that make the union safe: pack rules are **unlayered** so they intentionally win over Tailwind's `@layer utilities`, while `basecoat.generated.css` is `@layer components` so it always **loses** to our utilities. Corollaries: drop dead basecoat hook classes rather than keeping them (`alert` was doing nothing but forcing `<section>` + `data-title` structure), and never let a component's DOM shape exist only to satisfy a basecoat selector. Export the class strings from the `ui` object when a `Sc*` wrapper needs them reactively (`Alert.baseClass`/`Alert.variantClass`) instead of copying them into `webcomponents` — that copying is why `ScButton`/`ScBadge` have already drifted from `Button`/`Badge`.
- 2026-08-05: verifying a `modules/ui` styling change needs a **computed-style** check, not just a screenshot: `claude-in-chrome` isn't available in every session, but `~/Library/Caches/ms-playwright` already has browsers on this machine and the installed system Chrome can be driven with `chromium.launch({ channel: 'chrome' })` from a throwaway `npm i playwright` in `/tmp` (the cached chromium build lags what pw 1.62 wants, hence `channel`). Assert `gridTemplateColumns`/`columnGap`/computed colors and the *absence* of icon-vs-title overlap — Alert's missing grid was invisible in a screenshot because no docs example had an icon. Also re-measure with `data-style-pack` removed: that is the only way to see what a CLI consumer actually gets.
- 2026-08-05: **blocks live in their own `modules/blocks` sbt module**, not as a subpackage of `modules/ui`. Blocks are multi-file with nested directories, while `modules/ui` is one flat `.scala` + one sidecar per component, and "`modules/ui` is exactly what the CLI copies as components" is worth keeping true. Directories/packages are package-legal (`login01`) because Scala packages cannot contain hyphens, while the registry item name keeps the hyphen (`login-01`) in the sidecar. Laminar has no file-based routing, so a block's "page" file is a mountable `def apply(): HtmlElement` rather than a routed page — the one place blocks cannot match origin, which ships `page.tsx` with `target: app/login/page.tsx`.
- 2026-08-05: the block docs page's **Code tab fetches the generated `/registry/<name>.json` and renders `files[].content`** instead of using the hand-maintained `usageSource`-style string match components use. A 4-file, 325-line block cannot be kept in a string literal without drifting, and the registry JSON already holds the exact bytes the CLI would write. Verified: the rendered code block sizes match the registry file sizes exactly.
- 2026-08-05: `build-registry.mjs` takes a **`roots` array** (ui: flat, `scala:ui`, `ui/` prefix; blocks: recursive, `scala:block`, `blocks/` prefix) rather than one hardcoded directory, and per-file `type` falls back to the root's. `description`/`categories` are emitted only when the sidecar has them, so component items stay byte-identical — enforced by diffing all 60 against a pre-change baseline, not by eyeballing. A missing blocks root degrades to components-only instead of throwing.
- 2026-08-05: **`sidebar-01` was cut** from the blocks slice. It needs 14 Sidebar parts (`SidebarProvider`, `SidebarInset`, `SidebarTrigger`, `SidebarRail`, `SidebarGroupContent`, `SidebarMenuButton`, …); `modules/ui/Sidebar.scala` is 19 lines of six unrelated stubs with no provider, collapse state, inset or rail, against upstream `sidebar.tsx`'s 726 lines and 24 exports. Rebuilding Sidebar is its own sub-project.
- 2026-08-05: **`Button.apply` has no default variant/size** and therefore renders unstyled (20px tall, transparent, no padding), unlike upstream's cva `defaultVariants`. Always call `Button.of(_.variant(...), _.size(...), ...)`. This slipped through a compile *and* through computed-style assertions aimed at the card — only a screenshot caught it. Lesson: for a composition, measure the interactive controls, not just the container.
